1. Business Problem Definition
1.1 Problem Statement
Building on the Module 1 Vision Document, BrightCart’s core business problem is inefficient allocation of marketing, customer-retention, and operational resources caused by limited understanding of customer purchasing behaviour. BrightCart serves customers with different levels of recency, purchase frequency, monetary value, transaction volume, and product preferences. Treating these customers as a single population can result in broad strategies that do not reflect meaningful differences in customer value and behaviour.
The primary objective is to improve BrightCart’s operational efficiency by identifying and understanding distinct customer segments based on observed purchasing behaviour. The project will use the UCI Online Retail II dataset, which contains 1,067,371 real transaction records from a UK-based online retailer (Chen, 2019). RFM and behavioural features will support customer segmentation. Predictive inactivity/churn analysis and demand analysis will provide supporting insights, while prescriptive analytics will translate findings into segment-specific recommendations.
1.2 Problem-Identification Framework
Stage	Application to BrightCart
Define	Inefficient allocation of marketing, retention, and operational resources.
Gather Data	UCI Online Retail II transaction data.
Analyse	Assess purchasing behaviour and create customer-level RFM and behavioural features.
Identify Root Cause	No systematic workflow for converting transactions into actionable customer segments.
Set Objectives	Develop validated customer segments and decision-support insights.

2. Root Cause Analysis
2.1 Five Whys
Problem BrightCart’s marketing and operational resources are not allocated efficiently.
Why 1? Customers with substantially different purchasing behaviours may receive insufficiently differentiated strategies.
Why 2? BrightCart does not have clearly defined customer groups based on purchasing behaviour.
Why 3? Transaction data is not systematically transformed into customer-level behavioural profiles.
Why 4? There is no integrated workflow connecting data preparation, RFM analysis, clustering, and business decision support.
Why 5? BrightCart lacks a structured customer analytics capability that converts transaction data into actionable customer intelligence.
Root-cause finding: BrightCart lacks a systematic, data-driven customer segmentation process that converts transactional data into actionable customer intelligence.
2.2 KPIs Linked to the Root Cause
•	Revenue contribution by customer segment
•	Average monetary value and purchase frequency by segment
•	Customer recency and inactivity/churn risk
•	Segment size and stability
•	Demand analysis/forecast error
•	Data-quality exception rate and dashboard usability
3. Business Analytics Project Overview
3.1 Objectives and Scope
The project will:
1.	Validate and prepare the Online Retail II transaction dataset.
2.	Develop customer-level RFM and behavioural features.
3.	Identify and validate meaningful customer segments using K-Means clustering.
4.	Profile segment characteristics, size, and business value.
5.	Develop predictive analysis of customer inactivity/churn risk.
6.	Analyse demand patterns associated with customer segments.
7.	Translate results into segment-specific marketing, retention, and operational recommendations.
8.	Deliver an interactive Power BI decision-support dashboard.
9.	Apply privacy, fairness, explainability, and governance controls.
The project excludes real-time customer tracking, automated marketing campaigns, automated exclusion or pricing decisions, collection of new sensitive demographic information, and direct production-system integration. This boundary keeps the solution focused on responsible decision support.
3.2 Data and Analytical Methods
Data category	Example fields	Purpose
Transaction	InvoiceNo, InvoiceDate	Purchase activity and time patterns
Customer	CustomerID	Customer-level aggregation
Product	StockCode, Description	Product behaviour and preferences
Quantity	Quantity	Transaction volume
Financial	UnitPrice	Monetary value
Geography	Country	Descriptive/group-level analysis

Data preparation will address missing customer identifiers, cancelled transactions, duplicates, invalid quantities or prices, and unusual observations. Descriptive analytics will establish RFM measures; K-Means will provide the primary segmentation method; predictive modelling will examine inactivity/churn risk; time-based analysis will examine demand; and prescriptive analytics will translate results into actionable segment strategies.
3.3 Technical Architecture
The architecture follows the technical stack established in Module 1 and provides traceability from source transactions to business decisions.
Layer	Technology	Function
Source	UCI Online Retail II	Real transaction data
Storage	Azure Data Lake Storage	Secure raw and processed data storage
Ingestion	Python / Azure data pipeline	Data loading and controlled processing
Preparation	Python, Pandas, NumPy	Cleaning, transformation, feature engineering
Segmentation	Python, Scikit-learn	RFM analysis and K-Means clustering
Predictive analysis	Python, Scikit-learn	Inactivity/churn analysis
Explainability	SHAP / model-specific methods	Interpret predictive outputs
Visualization	Power BI	Interactive stakeholder dashboard
Versioning	Git/GitHub and DVC	Code, documentation, data/model tracking
Deployment	Azure	Controlled hosting and delivery

Architecture Flow
Stage 1	→	Stage 2	→	Stage 3
UCI Online Retail II	→	Azure Data Lake	→	Python / Pandas / NumPy
Customer Features + RFM	→	K-Means Segmentation	→	Predictive Inactivity/Churn
Demand Analysis	→	Prescriptive Recommendations	→	Power BI Dashboard

4. Agile Methodology and Delivery Plan
The project will use an iterative Agile approach based on short sprints, continuous validation, stakeholder feedback, and incremental delivery. This reduces the risk of discovering major data, modelling, or usability problems only at the end of the project.
Sprint	Focus	Key deliverables
1	Foundation and data assessment	GitHub repository, environment, data dictionary, data-quality assessment
2	Data preparation and RFM	Cleaning pipeline, processed dataset, RFM features
3	Customer segmentation	K-Means models, cluster evaluation, segment profiles
4	Predictive and prescriptive analytics	Inactivity analysis, demand insights, recommendations
5	Dashboard and validation	Power BI dashboard, XAI/fairness assessment, final documentation

4.1 GitHub Project Board
The GitHub Kanban board will use Backlog → Ready → In Progress → Review → Done. Tickets will be mapped to the repository structure established in Module 1.
Ticket	Sprint	Output
Create repository and environment	1	GitHub structure and requirements.txt
Create data dictionary and quality report	1	Preliminary documentation
Clean transactions and calculate RFM	2	Processed customer dataset
Evaluate K-Means configurations	3	Validated segmentation
Profile customer segments	3	Segment profiles
Develop inactivity model and demand analysis	4	Predictive and demand outputs
Develop segment recommendations	4	Prescriptive outputs
Build Power BI dashboard	5	Interactive dashboard
Complete XAI, fairness and final validation	5	Responsible-AI documentation

