5.1 Technical RAID Log
Type	Item	Impact	Mitigation / Action
Risk	Missing CustomerID	Reduced customer-level analysis	Quantify missingness and document treatment
Risk	Cancelled transactions	Distorted RFM values	Define and document transaction-cleaning rules
Risk	Poor cluster separation	Weak segments	Compare K values and validation metrics
Risk	Privacy exposure	Compliance/reputational risk	Minimize identifiers and restrict access
Risk	Model bias	Potential unfair outcomes	Conduct fairness/error analysis
Assumption	Historical data represents useful behaviour	May affect generalizability	Use temporal validation where appropriate
Issue	Historical rather than real-time data	Limited real-time decisions	State limitation clearly
Dependency	Azure environment	Pipeline delays	Configure environment early
Dependency	Power BI availability	Dashboard delay	Begin dashboard design during Sprint 3

6. Privacy, Fairness and Compliance Requirements
6.1 Data Privacy Plan
The project will apply data minimization, purpose limitation, controlled access, pseudonymization, secure storage, and appropriate retention. Customer identifiers will only be used when required for analytical aggregation. Stakeholder dashboards will prioritize aggregated information rather than exposing unnecessary customer-level identifiers. Access to raw and processed data will be role-based, and data-processing activities will be documented for traceability.
6.2 Fairness Requirements
The Online Retail II dataset contains limited protected demographic information; therefore, comprehensive demographic fairness testing is not possible with the available data. This limitation will be explicitly documented. The project will avoid using sensitive characteristics as segmentation variables and will examine whether predictive errors or outcomes differ across relevant groups where appropriate information exists.
Where suitable group information becomes available for the predictive component, Demographic Parity and Equalized Odds may be evaluated alongside group-level precision, recall, and error rates. The purpose is to identify potentially disproportionate outcomes rather than impose a fairness criterion that is inappropriate for the business context.
6.3 Explainability Framework
•	K-Means: explain clusters using RFM distributions, cluster centroids, feature comparisons, and segment profiles.
•	Predictive models: use SHAP or model-specific feature importance and error analysis.
•	Dashboard: distinguish observed historical measures, predictions, and recommendations.
•	Business users will receive explanations in non-technical language and retain responsibility for decisions.
6.4 Regulatory Compliance Checklist
Requirement	Project control
Data protection principles	Purpose limitation, minimization, security, accuracy, and retention controls
Privacy	Restrict identifiers; use pseudonymized or aggregated outputs
Transparency	Document methods, assumptions, limitations, and model outputs
Accountability	Assign analytical and business ownership
Fairness	Conduct appropriate group/error analysis and document limitations
Human oversight	No autonomous consequential customer decisions
Retention	Retain information only for the approved analytical purpose

GDPR/data-protection principles will provide the primary privacy reference where personal data is processed. CCPA and HIPAA are not treated as primary frameworks because the selected context is a retail project rather than a US consumer-law or healthcare implementation; obligations would be reassessed if scope or jurisdiction changes.
7. Conclusion
This Project Overview Report converts the Module 1 BrightCart vision into an executable delivery plan. The root-cause analysis demonstrates that the underlying business problem is not simply the absence of customer segmentation, but the lack of a systematic process for converting transaction data into actionable customer intelligence.
The proposed architecture connects data ingestion, preparation, RFM feature engineering, K-Means segmentation, predictive analysis, demand analysis, prescriptive recommendations, and Power BI visualization. The Agile sprint plan provides incremental delivery, while the stakeholder plan and RAID log establish accountability and risk management. Privacy, fairness, explainability, and governance controls are embedded throughout the project.
The intended outcome is a reproducible, responsible analytics capability that enables BrightCart to understand customer differences and make better-informed decisions about marketing, retention, and operational resource allocation.
